\title{Structural and Enumerative Results for Frankl's\\Union-Closed Sets Conjecture}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 29, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A finite family of finite sets is union-closed when it contains the union of every pair of its members. Frankl's conjecture asserts that every such family with a nonempty member has an element occurring in at least half of its members. We develop a self-contained collection of partial results and complementary viewpoints. An explicit injection proves the conjecture whenever the family contains a singleton. Combining this injection with a finite residual classification proves the conjecture for every family on a three-element universe. Every nonempty finite union-closed family contains the union of all its members and is therefore a finite join-semilattice with a greatest element. Finally, exact double counting on the Boolean cube gives total subset size $n2^{n-1}$ and average subset size $n/2$, identifying the full Boolean lattice as an equality example for the benchmark $\frac12\log_2|\F|$ in Reimer's average-size inequality. We state algorithms for testing closure, abundance, the three-point result, and the cube identities, together with their complexity.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Let $U$ be a finite set and let $\F\subseteq\Pset(U)$ be a finite family. The family is union-closed if
\[
 A,B\in\F\quad\Longrightarrow\quad A\cup B\in\F.
\]
Frankl's union-closed sets conjecture states that if at least one member of $\F$ is nonempty, then some element of $U$ belongs to at least half the members. If
\[
 d_{\F}(x)=|\{A\in\F:x\in A\}|,
\]
then the desired condition is $2d_{\F}(x)\ge |\F|$.

Union-closure controls upward combinations, but it does not require intersections, subsets, or complements. This asymmetry makes the conjecture resistant to elementary averaging. The results below isolate four settings in which closure has a transparent consequence: families containing a singleton, all families on three points, the order structure of arbitrary nonempty families, and the full Boolean lattice.

The scope is precise. We do not claim the general conjecture or prove Reimer's general average-size inequality. We prove the exact Boolean-cube identity that realizes equality in its benchmark. Likewise, the complete finite theorem concerns a three-element universe; larger finite bounds are discussed as future work.

\section{Definitions and preliminary counting}
\begin{definition}[Union-closed family]
A finite family $\F$ of finite subsets of $U$ is \emph{union-closed} if $A\cup B\in\F$ for every $A,B\in\F$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Frequency and abundance]
For $x\in U$, set
\[
 \F_x=\{A\in\F:x\in A\},\qquad
 \F_{\bar x}=\{A\in\F:x\notin A\}.
\]
The frequency of $x$ is $d_{\F}(x)=|\F_x|$. The element $x$ is \emph{abundant} if $|\F|\le 2|\F_x|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Frankl property]
A family $\F$ has the Frankl property if there exists an element $x$ lying in at least one member of $\F$ such that $x$ is abundant.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Frequency partition]
For every finite family $\F$ and element $x$,
\[
 |\F|=|\F_x|+|\F_{\bar x}|.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Every member either contains $x$ or avoids it, and the two possibilities are disjoint.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[An abundant point occurs]
If $\F$ is nonempty and $x$ is abundant, then some member of $\F$ contains $x$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Otherwise $|\F_x|=0$, while abundance gives $|\F|\le0$, contradicting nonemptiness.
\end{proof}

\section{The singleton injection}
\begin{theorem}[Singleton Injection Theorem]
Let $\F$ be a finite union-closed family. If $\{a\}\in\F$, then $a$ is abundant:
\[
 |\F|\le 2|\F_a|.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $\mathcal S=\F_{\bar a}$ and $\mathcal T=\F_a$. Define
\[
 \Phi:\mathcal S\longrightarrow\mathcal T,
 \qquad \Phi(A)=A\cup\{a\}.
\]
If $A\in\mathcal S$, then $A,\{a\}\in\F$, so union-closure puts $\Phi(A)$ in $\F$; it contains $a$, hence belongs to $\mathcal T$. If $A,B\in\mathcal S$ and $A\cup\{a\}=B\cup\{a\}$, deleting $a$ from both sides gives $A=B$. Thus $\Phi$ is injective and $|\mathcal S|\le|\mathcal T|$. The frequency partition now yields
\[
 |\F|=|\mathcal S|+|\mathcal T|\le2|\mathcal T|=2|\F_a|.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Singleton case of Frankl's property]
A union-closed family containing a singleton has the Frankl property.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The singleton's element occurs in the family and is abundant by the theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
The family
\[
 \F=\{\varnothing,\{a\},\{b,c\},\{a,b,c\}\}
\]
is union-closed. Adjoining $a$ pairs $\varnothing$ with $\{a\}$ and $\{b,c\}$ with $\{a,b,c\}$, so $a$ occurs in exactly half the members.
\end{example}

The theorem supplies an explicit matching rather than only an inequality. The hypothesis that the domain sets avoid $a$ makes adjoining $a$ reversible. Replacing the singleton by an unrestricted larger minimal set does not retain this feature in general.

\section{The three-element universe}
Let $U=\{0,1,2\}$. Its power set has eight members, so there are $2^8=256$ candidate families.

\begin{lemma}[No-singleton three-point lemma]
Suppose $|U|=3$ and $\F\subseteq\Pset(U)$ is union-closed, contains a nonempty member, and contains no singleton. Then some $x\in U$ satisfies
\[
 |\F|\le2|\F_x|.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
This is a finite exhaustive classification. Encode the eight subsets of $U$ by masks $0,\ldots,7$ and each family by an eight-bit mask $0,\ldots,255$. For each candidate, require that the bit for $A\cup B$ is present whenever the bits for $A$ and $B$ are present. Reject candidates with no nonempty member or with any singleton mask $1$, $2$, or $4$. For every remaining candidate, count the selected masks containing each of the three coordinates. Direct enumeration gives
\[
 \max_{x\in U}2|\F_x|\ge|\F|.
\]
Every labeled family occurs exactly once in this list, and relabeling preserves closure and frequencies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Frankl's conjecture on three points]
Every union-closed family of subsets of a three-element universe containing a nonempty member has the Frankl property.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If the family contains a singleton, apply the Singleton Injection Theorem. If it contains no singleton, apply the no-singleton three-point lemma. In the latter case the family is nonempty, so an abundant point must occur in some member.
\end{proof}

The proof separates a dimension-free structural argument from a genuinely finite residue. A candidate can be tested using at most $8^2$ pairwise closure checks and $3\cdot8$ incidence checks.

\section{Join-semilattice structure}
Order $\F$ by inclusion. In a union-closed family, $A\cup B$ is the join of $A$ and $B$ and remains in $\F$.

\begin{theorem}[Greatest-Member Theorem]
Let $\F$ be a nonempty finite union-closed family and define
\[
 T=\bigcup_{A\in\F}A.
\]
Then $T\in\F$, and every $A\in\F$ satisfies $A\subseteq T$. Thus $T$ is the greatest member of $\F$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Enumerate $\F$ as $A_1,\ldots,A_m$. Starting with $T_1=A_1$, define $T_{k+1}=T_k\cup A_{k+1}$. An induction using union-closure gives $T_k\in\F$ for all $k$. The last term is
\[
 T_m=A_1\cup\cdots\cup A_m=T,
\]
so $T\in\F$. Every member is included in the displayed union.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Every nonempty finite union-closed family is a finite join-semilattice with a greatest element.
\end{corollary}

For each $x$, the subfamily $\F_x$ is upward closed within this ordered set: if $A\in\F_x$ and $A\subseteq B\in\F$, then $B\in\F_x$. Frankl's property can therefore be viewed as asking for an incidence filter of size at least $|\F|/2$. Ground elements that determine identical filters are indistinguishable and may be identified in a separating reduction.

\section{The Boolean cube}
Let $U$ have $n$ elements and consider the full Boolean lattice $\Pset(U)$.

\begin{lemma}[Cardinality of the Boolean lattice]
If $|U|=n$, then $|\Pset(U)|=2^n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Each of the $n$ elements is independently included or excluded.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Half-cube frequency]
For $x\in U$, exactly $2^{n-1}$ subsets contain $x$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Require $x$ to be present and choose freely among the remaining $n-1$ elements. Equivalently, adjoining $x$ bijects subsets avoiding $x$ with subsets containing it.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Boolean-Cube Incidence Identity]
For an $n$-element universe,
\[
 \sum_{A\subseteq U}|A|=n2^{n-1},
\]
with the right-hand side interpreted as zero when $n=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Count incidences $(x,A)$ satisfying $x\in A\subseteq U$. Counting by subsets gives $\sum_{A\subseteq U}|A|$. Counting by elements gives $2^{n-1}$ incidences for each of $n$ elements when $n\ge1$, hence $n2^{n-1}$. When $n=0$, the only subset is empty and both sides vanish.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact cube average]
For the full Boolean lattice,
\[
 2\sum_{A\subseteq U}|A|=n|\Pset(U)|.
\]
Consequently its average member size is $n/2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The two sides are $2n2^{n-1}$ and $n2^n$ by the preceding results. Dividing the identity by $2|\Pset(U)|$ gives the average.
\end{proof}

Reimer's average-size inequality gives the benchmark
\[
 \frac12\log_2|\F|
\]
for a finite union-closed family. In the Boolean cube, $|\F|=2^n$, so this benchmark is $n/2$, exactly the computed average. Thus the cube realizes equality. This observation uses the stated general inequality only for context; the equality computation itself is the elementary identity above.

\begin{corollary}[Frankl property of a nontrivial Boolean lattice]
If $U$ is nonempty, every element of $U$ is abundant in $\Pset(U)$, and $\Pset(U)$ has the Frankl property.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
For every $x\in U$,
\[
 2d_{\Pset(U)}(x)=2\cdot2^{n-1}=2^n=|\Pset(U)|.
\]
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms and complexity}
Represent a subset of $\{0,\ldots,n-1\}$ by an integer bit mask. Union becomes bitwise OR, membership is a bit test, and cardinality is population count.

\paragraph{Pairwise union-closure test.}
Store the $m$ family masks in a hash set. For every ordered pair $(A,B)$, check whether $A\mathbin{\mathrm{OR}}B$ is present. This takes $O(m^2)$ expected time and $O(m)$ space when fixed-width bit operations are constant time.

\paragraph{Incidence frequency scan.}
Initialize $n$ counters. For each member and each coordinate, increment the corresponding counter when the bit is present. Return all coordinates $x$ satisfying $2d_{\F}(x)\ge m$. The direct complexity is $O(nm)$ time and $O(n)$ space.

\paragraph{Three-point residual enumeration.}
Enumerate all $256$ family masks, test closure, require a nonempty member, remove singleton-containing cases if studying the residue, and scan frequencies. More generally, a direct search on $n$ points examines $2^{2^n}$ families and is therefore doubly exponential. For $n=4$ there are already $2^{16}=65{,}536$ candidates, making symmetry and structural reductions increasingly valuable.

\paragraph{Boolean-cube counter.}
Enumerate all $2^n$ subset masks, sum their population counts, and compare with $n2^{n-1}$. Coordinate frequencies may be accumulated simultaneously. This costs $O(2^n)$ machine-word operations with constant-time population count, or $O(n2^n)$ elementary bit operations.

\section{Applications and interpretation}
Union-closed families model admissible states stable under aggregation. A set can encode permissions, active features, or accumulated knowledge; union combines two states. The greatest-member theorem says that repeated aggregation reaches an admissible state containing every feature that occurs anywhere. The singleton theorem says that if an isolated feature is itself admissible, then it occurs in at least half of all admissible states. The Boolean cube models completely independent binary features and exhibits exact half-frequency.

The incidence identity
\[
 \sum_{A\in\F}|A|=\sum_{x\in U}d_{\F}(x)
\]
connects average member size to total coordinate frequency in every family. The hard step in the general conjecture is to turn aggregate information into a single frequency at least $|\F|/2$. The cube accomplishes this through perfect symmetry; the singleton case accomplishes it through an injection.

\section{Discussion and future work}
The present results exhibit three proof paradigms: injection, structural decomposition followed by finite enumeration, and double counting. The singleton injection is explicit and independent of universe size. The three-point theorem reserves exhaustive search for the no-singleton residue. The Boolean-cube count explains exact equality in a symmetric extremal family.

Natural continuations include the following.
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item On four points, reduce the $65{,}536$ candidates using singletons, separating families, complements, and isomorphism classes under permutations.
\item For families with at most fifty members, develop the separating reductions, frequency constraints, and finite configuration analysis needed for the Bo\v{s}njak--Markovi\'c bound.
\item Package the order-theoretic viewpoint as an abstract finite join-semilattice and express element frequencies through incidence filters.
\item Identify ground elements having identical incidence columns and quotient by this relation while preserving union-closure and frequencies.
\item Develop finite Shannon entropy, the chain rule, Shearer's inequality, and the incidence-vector argument needed for a self-contained proof of the general average-size bound.
\item Study equality and stability: determine whether near equality in average size forces resemblance to a Boolean subcube.
\item Seek further explicit injections for carefully constrained small minimal members, while avoiding the false unrestricted smallest-member heuristic.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
A nonempty finite union-closed family always has a greatest member. If it contains a singleton, an explicit injection proves that the singleton's element occurs in at least half the family. On three points, this structural theorem plus a finite no-singleton classification proves Frankl's property completely. In the full Boolean lattice, each coordinate occurs exactly half the time, total subset size is $n2^{n-1}$, and average size is $n/2$.

These results distinguish the mechanisms responsible for abundance in several natural regimes: repeated joins, reversible singleton adjunction, bounded classification, and Boolean symmetry. The general conjecture asks for a mechanism surviving when none of these special features is immediately available.

\end{document}

