# Finding a Halfway Element

A union-closed family is stable under merging: whenever two sets are present, their union is present too. Frankl’s conjecture predicts that some element appears in at least half the family. Start with the [basic background on union-closed families](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Union-closed_sets_conjecture), then explore the mechanisms below.

## 1. Build and test a family
Use the three-point builder to select any of the eight subsets, test closure, and inspect coordinate frequencies.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the precise three-point theorem and proof architecture</summary>
Every union-closed family on a three-element universe with a nonempty member has an abundant element. If a singleton occurs, an explicit injection settles the claim. Otherwise, the finite residue is exhausted among the $2^8=256$ possible families; every eligible no-singleton family passes the frequency inequality $2d(x)\ge |\mathcal F|$.
</details>

{{demo:1}}

## 2. Watch the singleton injection
The key structural move sends every set avoiding $a$ to its union with $\{a\}$. Because the family contains $\{a\}$ and is union-closed, every image stays inside the family.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Why is the map injective?</summary>
On sets avoiding $a$, the operation $A\mapsto A\cup\{a\}$ is reversible: delete $a$ to recover $A$. Thus the avoiding class injects into the containing class, proving that at least half the members contain $a$.
</details>

{{visualization:1}}

## 3. Enter the Boolean cube
The full power set has $2^n$ members. Every coordinate occurs in exactly $2^{n-1}$ of them, and double counting incidences gives $\sum_{A\subseteq U}|A|=n2^{n-1}$.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the double-counting proof</summary>
Count pairs $(x,A)$ with $x\in A\subseteq U$. Counting first by $A$ gives $\sum_A|A|$. Counting first by $x$ gives $n2^{n-1}$, because the other $n-1$ coordinates are free. Consequently the average subset size is $n/2$.
</details>

## 4. See the order structure
Inclusion turns a union-closed family into a finite join-semilattice. Repeated unions produce the greatest member, the union of the entire family.

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the greatest-member argument</summary>
List the nonempty finite family as $A_1,\ldots,A_m$. Closure places $A_1\cup A_2$ in the family, then $(A_1\cup A_2)\cup A_3$, and so forth. The final set is $ igcup_iA_i$, and every member lies below it.
</details>

## 5. Reproduce the complete numerical study
Run the integrated demonstration for the injection, all three-point families, and Boolean cubes through dimension eight.

{{demo:0}}

{{algorithm:2}}

For further context on the surrounding extremal method, see [entropy in information theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Entropy_(information_theory)) and [partially ordered sets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Partially_ordered_set).

